1. What is a Solaris Cluster?

A Solaris Cluster is a group of two or more Solaris servers connected together to provide High Availability (HA).

High Availability means:

If one server fails, another server automatically takes over so the application keeps running.

2. Why do we need a Cluster?

Imagine a bank has only one server.

Customers
    │
    ▼
Server A

If Server A crashes because of a hardware problem,

❌ Internet Banking stops.

❌ ATM services stop.

❌ Customers cannot transfer money.

This causes downtime and business loss.

3. Solution: Solaris Cluster

Instead of one server, the company uses two servers.

Customers
     │
     ▼
-------------------
|                 |
Server A      Server B
(Active)      (Standby)

Normally:

Server A runs the application.
Server B waits in standby mode.

If Server A fails,

➡️ Server B automatically starts the application.

Customers continue using the service with very little interruption.

This is called Failover (Automatically moving an application from a failed server to a healthy server).

4. Components of a Solaris Cluster
a) Node

A Node is simply a server.

Example:

Node 1 = Server A

Node 2 = Server B

A cluster must have at least two nodes.

b) Shared Storage

Both servers use the same storage.

          Shared Storage
          (Disk)
          /      \
         /        \
   Server A    Server B

Why?

Because the application data should be available even if one server fails.

Example:

Bank customer data is stored in shared storage.

If Server A crashes,

Server B can still access the same customer data.

c) Heartbeat

The servers continuously check whether the other server is alive.

This is called a Heartbeat.

Example:

Every few seconds,

Server A → "Are you alive?"

Server B → "Yes"

Server A → "Okay"

If there is no response,

Server A → "Are you alive?"

(No reply)

Server A → "Server B has failed."

The cluster immediately starts failover.

5. Solaris Cluster vs Solaris Zones

| Solaris Zones                                        | Solaris Cluster                          |
| ---------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| Creates multiple isolated environments on one server | Connects multiple servers together       |
| Used for application isolation                       | Used for High Availability               |
| Shares one Solaris kernel                            | Uses two or more separate servers        |
| Saves hardware resources                             | Prevents downtime during server failures |


